      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "WHY THE ROUTE IS `smt-clausal` AND NOT `smt-term-level`. axeyum decided this through CNF and returned a DRAT refutation, which it then re-checked. It did NOT take the term-level enumeration route: the fpa2bv lowering of a binary16 add-vs-multiply miter introduces intermediate bit-width well past the 20-bit budget `TERM_LEVEL_CERT_BITS` allows, so the exhaustive-evaluation certificate is unavailable here even though the INPUT domain is only 16 bits. The exhaustive enumeration that is available is the second evidence row, and it is not axeyum's IR evaluator -- it is LLVM's APFloat. Naming the route `smt-term-level` would have made the footprint incomparable with the facts that really did take that route.\n\nWHAT THE FOOTPRINT IS ADMITTING. `axeyum-fp`'s `add` and `mul` are documented in their own doc comments as \"validated -- not formally proven -- bit-blasters\", differentially validated against native f32/f64 and rustc_apfloat's Quad. So the SMT route's answer is only as good as that lowering, which is exactly why the APFloat enumeration is here: it evaluates the SAME proposition without going through the lowering at all. Two routes agreeing is the evidence; one route asserting would not be.\n\nSCOPE, STATED PRECISELY. Exhaustive at binary16 (2^16 inputs, every one enumerated). The sibling F:fp32-doubling-add-equals-mul-two carries the binary32 instance, where the solver route is symbolic. The same identity was also enumerated exhaustively at fp8 E5M2 and bfloat16 in the same run; it is asserted here only for binary16 because a fact is a proposition, and \"for every format\" is a different and stronger one this lane did not establish.",
